You open the same three directories every week. You also open enough
terminal windows to forget which one you set aside for experiments.
Two small changes would help: a short name for an inspection command
and a prompt that says which session is the experiment. Neither change
needs to affect the other windows, and neither needs to survive
tomorrow. Start by deciding what should not change.

\begin{objectives}
\item Try an alias and a short prompt in a child shell.
\item Distinguish session state, inherited settings, and startup files.
\item Test a candidate startup change without installing it, and plan a rollback.
\end{objectives}

\section{A room with its own light switch}\label{sec:43-child}

Open a spare terminal window. In that window type \cmd|sh| first if
your normal shell does not read POSIX shell syntax. The practice
commands below use that syntax except where explicitly marked Bash or
Zsh. Check that Bash is available, then start it without its usual
login or interactive startup files. If the first command cannot
locate Bash, skip the second command and start a spare child
\cmd|sh| instead. The plain prompt, alias and environment examples
use POSIX syntax. Skip the nested Bash trial unless you can use the
Zsh alternative discussed there.

\begin{terminal}
command -v bash
bash --noprofile --norc -i
\end{terminal}

\cmd|command -v| asks how the current shell resolves a name. Here we
want a Bash executable. The long options are Bash options:
\cmd|--noprofile| skips profile files; \cmd|--norc| skips the interactive
run-command file; \cmd|-i| requests an interactive shell. This is a
child shell (a separate shell process), not a different terminal.
It can still inherit exported values from its parent. It also starts
in the parent's working directory. Do not mistake it for a security
sandbox: it can access the same files you can.

Instead of using home files, make a new practice directory. If the
assignment or \cmd|cd| fails, stop and do not enter the examples that
write there. \cmd|mktemp -d| makes a fresh directory; the six Xs in the
template are replaced. \cmd|${TMPDIR:-/tmp}| uses the temporary
directory in the environment or falls back to \FilePath{/tmp}. The
value was created in this child, so this chapter's cleanup is done
before typing \cmd|exit|.

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/utp43.XXXXXX") &&
    cd "$lab" && lab=$PWD
pwd
PS1='practice> '
printf '%s\n' 'This command runs inside the practice shell.'
\end{terminal}

The final assignment keeps the absolute working directory for cleanup.
\cmd|pwd| prints the working directory. Assigning to \cmd|PS1| in Bash
or a POSIX-style interactive shell sets the main prompt. The quotes
hold the trailing space; the space is useful when visually separating
the prompt from what you type. Do not type the prompt itself. In a
screen reader, listen for whether the separator is announced in a
helpful way; not all punctuation is spoken under all settings.
Figure~\ref{fig:43-boundary} shows why \cmd|exit| will later recover
the parent prompt without undoing effects on files.

\input{figures/chapter-43/boundary.tikz}

There are two questions to ask about a proposed change. Who should
see it? How long should it last? If it affects only the current
shell, the answer to the first question may be ``only this tab.''
Even that is an approximation: a tab may contain a parent shell and
a child shell, while only one shell has the new setting. If a program
you start needs it, export an environment value. If tomorrow's shell
needs it, arrange for the correct startup file to define it then.
These are separate choices, not three spellings of the same thing.

A login shell can read personal files before you see any prompt.
Distinguish slow startup from a slow prompt: compare the wait
at first launch with the wait after pressing Enter on an empty
input line at the main prompt. Compare again in the clean child.
The timing is a clue, not proof of which particular command is
slow. If a single addition was made recently, undo that addition
first rather than rebuilding the shell environment from a
template. A change that seems small can depend on a network
service or on a folder on a removable volume; neither is
guaranteed to be ready when the prompt appears.

\begin{quicktable}{lY}
\toprule
Change & What inspecting a second shell may reveal \\
\midrule
Alias or prompt & Definitions made in the first shell need not appear in the second. \\
Exported setting & Child processes can inherit it; an existing sibling window does not acquire it. \\
Edited startup file & Future applicable shells may read it; existing ones keep their state. \\
File written by a command & Both shells can encounter it if they can access the path. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{A name that still lets you see the command}\label{sec:43-alias}

Try shortening a read-only command. \cmd|ls -l| requests a long
listing; \cmd|-d| lists a directory operand itself rather than
its contents. These options make \cmd|inspect .| a way to inspect the
current directory entry. The alias expands the first command word
before ordinary execution; it does not erase the operand.

\begin{terminal}
alias inspect='ls -ld'
alias inspect
inspect .
command ls -ld .
\end{terminal}

Both listing commands should report the same directory entry. The
printed permission, owner, size, and date fields will depend on your
system. The invocation \cmd|command ls -ld .| puts \cmd|command| in
the first command-word position, so any ordinary alias called
\cmd|ls| does not expand there, and the \cmd|command| utility also
suppresses shell function lookup for its operand. This is not a
promise about the executable found through your \cmd|PATH|.
If a name does not behave as expected, consult
Chapter~\ref{ch:36} before changing \cmd|PATH|.
\index{alias@\texttt{alias}}\index{unalias@\texttt{unalias}}\index{aliases}

\begin{terminal}
unalias inspect
if command -v inspect; then
    printf '%s\n' 'Another definition exists.'
else
    printf '%s\n' 'The shortcut is gone.'
fi
\end{terminal}

The test prints the resolution if one exists and chooses a message
from the exit status. It does not execute \cmd|inspect|. Avoid
redefining familiar names for unfamiliar behavior: your hands will
remember the spelling on a machine that lacks your definition. In
particular an alias that asks before deleting files is not a backup
or a portable safety rule. The next person's shell and scripts need
not read your alias. Pick names that announce intent and leave
standard names intact. This also reduces the work of explaining a
shared recipe; write the underlying command, not your alias, in team
instructions.

A good alias is easy to explain from its name. A private habit
can still be useful, but it shouldn't be essential to finishing
a task on another person's computer. If lookup shows a collision,
pick another name. Avoid layers of aliases whose definitions
depend on each other. When a routine outgrows an alias, the
interesting requirement is usually not brevity but giving the
routine a place to check inputs and handle failure.

An alias is useful when the pieces that vary can simply follow the
replacement. Consider a shortcut that always verifies a file before
copying it to an argument-dependent destination. An alias cannot put
one user-supplied argument into two independent places in the way
that recipe needs. Use a small script with explicit arguments and
checks instead (Chapter~\ref{ch:30}). The boundary in
Figure~\ref{fig:43-lookup} is the practical difference: command text
changes before execution; it is not a new executable file.

\input{figures/chapter-43/lookup.tikz}

\begin{dialoguebox}{A safer name}
\Brian{Couldn't I make the ordinary delete command ask every time?}
\Stephen{An alias could add a question in the interactive shell.}
\Brian{Then why not count that as a safety net?}
\Stephen{It works only where the alias takes effect. I was too quick to call that protection.}
\Brian{So a script could use the same spelling and never ask.}
\Stephen{Yes. Use a distinct name for convenience, and don't rely on a prompt to recover deleted files.}
\Brian{I'd rather learn which files I'm acting on than remember which machine asks.}
\DialogueConclusion{A shortcut is not a safety rule.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{A setting that travels, and a prompt that stays}

Use a made-up variable instead of experimenting with your search
path. Set it, then use \cmd|sh -c| to run the same small string of
shell code in a new process twice. Single quotes around that string
leave the expansion to the child; the double quotes around the
expansion inside the string keep its result in one argument.
\cmd|${UTP43_NOTE-unset}| means use the word \texttt{unset} only
when the variable is unset. If the variable name was already in
the environment, the initial \cmd|unset| clears it in this child
Bash only.

\begin{terminal}
unset UTP43_NOTE
UTP43_NOTE=visible
sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "${UTP43_NOTE-unset}"'
export UTP43_NOTE
sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "${UTP43_NOTE-unset}"'
unset UTP43_NOTE
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{unset} and then \texttt{visible}. No confirmation
appears after the final \cmd|unset|; it removes the variable from
this shell and the environment of children launched afterwards.
An export is not a way to push a setting into a program already
running. Nor is a shell variable necessarily a good place for a
secret: a command line, an environment value or its expansion can show
up in diagnostic information or history, depending on how it is used.
This is deliberately a made-up word.

Give a shell a prompt it can print simply. A plain word tends to work
across terminals and assistive tools. A prompt that runs a command to
collect data every time can make a slow operation look like a frozen
shell. It can also reveal details of the directory or host on a shared
screen. A prompt is a cue, not proof of context: before acting on
relative paths, verify with \cmd|pwd|. A remote terminal that looks
just like a local one still runs commands where the shell lives.

\subsection{A useful prompt can be plain}

Choose the prompt for the most costly mistake you actually make.
If you keep confusing a test shell with the ordinary shell, use
a word such as \texttt{practice}, not the same prompt in two
different colors. If the problem is several indistinguishable
remote tabs, tag the window and verify the machine before work;
do not spend time encoding machine identity in a decorative
glyph. Avoid putting a whole deeply nested path in the prompt
if it leaves only a few characters where the command is edited.
A short prompt and a deliberate \cmd|pwd| on arrival is a
reasonable combination. An application tab title is a separate
cue; its settings are not inherited by a child shell through
\cmd|PS1|.

To see how the separator changes the experience, try two prompts
in sequence, each on its own line. One has an explicit word and
colon so that it reads well in plain text. You can return to the
first practice prompt immediately. This doesn't alter the
terminal's font, contrast, or cursor behavior.

\begin{terminal}
PS1='REVIEW: '
pwd
PS1='practice> '
\end{terminal}

Color sequences and dynamic elements are shell-specific. Bash,
for example, documents backslash sequences in its prompt and a
way to bracket nonprinting parts, while Zsh uses different prompt
escapes. If a prompt displays but long commands wrap in the wrong
place, test again with a simple text prompt. The line editor
may have counted terminal controls as visible columns. If plain
text fixes the symptom, don't change the working directory or
terminal width to hide it. There is no need to repair a prompt
with escapes to make this chapter's workflow useful.

\section{Test a proposed startup setting}

A \FirstUse{startup file} is a file a shell reads automatically under
specified circumstances. It may include commands with side effects.
Do not source a file you did not inspect, and do not install a copied
startup file as a shortcut to learning it. Even a shell's syntax
check is only a grammar check: valid commands can still fail, run
slowly, or do unwanted work. Our candidate contains only two known
assignments of state: a prompt and an alias.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' "PS1='review> '" "alias inspect='ls -ld'" \
    > ./candidate.sh
cat ./candidate.sh
sh -n ./candidate.sh
\end{terminal}

The backslash at the end of the line continues the command and is
not followed by spaces; \cmd|>| writes a new file or truncates an
existing one. It is safe here because this is your fresh fixture
and the name is reserved for the candidate. \cmd|sh -n| reads
commands without executing them and reports syntax errors to
standard error. Silence with success status is expected. The
separate \cmd|cat| check matters because valid syntax alone does
not describe intent. The file is available as
\FilePath{listings/chapter-43/candidate.sh}.

For Bash-specific startup syntax, check it with \cmd|bash -n| but
do not claim that a successful result makes it portable. To see
the effect of our small candidate, make a new interactive Bash and
explicitly read the file inside it. Run each of the following
commands separately once you see a prompt. In particular, reading
the file and invoking its alias on the same input line can fail
because the shell reads that line before the alias exists.

\begin{terminal}
bash --noprofile --norc -i
. ./candidate.sh
inspect .
exit
\end{terminal}

The dot is the POSIX command to read shell code into the current
shell. The slash in \cmd|./candidate.sh| selects the file by path
rather than letting the shell search for it. Expect \texttt{review>}
in the nested child and \texttt{practice>} again after \cmd|exit|.
The candidate still exists. The alias does not remain in the
outer practice shell. Zsh users can try the same experiment with
\cmd|zsh -f| in place of the Bash invocation; \cmd|-f| skips most
startup files, not necessarily the system's \texttt{zshenv}. The
plain prompt works in that shell too.

\input{figures/chapter-43/startup.tikz}

Figure~\ref{fig:43-startup} separates editing, checking, and trying.
None of these steps automatically installs the candidate. Check the
following routes before deciding a real file is the right place.
Login and interactive are two distinct properties: the former is how
the shell was invoked, the latter is whether the shell expects
an interactive conversation. Terminal applications can choose
how to launch the shell. The existence of a particular filename in
your home directory does not prove that your terminal uses it.

There may also be a site-wide file, read before yours, that you
should not edit. And one startup file can explicitly read
another. That is why reading a manual is only the first step:
compare the ordering it documents with the files that actually
exist and the commands in those files. A terminal set to start a
login shell can work perfectly even when a differently launched
shell misses an alias. The two launches are simply following
different instructions. Noninteractive scripts should put
necessary settings in the script or document their inputs; avoid
depending on a subtle accident of interactive setup.

\begin{quicktable}{lY}
\toprule
Shell & Where to check for user-level interactive settings \\
\midrule
Bash & Non-login interactive normally reads \texttt{.bashrc}.
Login shells take a profile route; a profile can explicitly read
\texttt{.bashrc}. See the startup-files manual for ordering. \\
Zsh & Interactive shells normally read \texttt{.zshrc} under
\texttt{ZDOTDIR} (home if unset); login shells also have a separate
profile and login sequence. \\
POSIX sh & For interactive shells, check the \texttt{ENV} mechanism
and your implementation's manual; do not assume \texttt{.shrc}
is universal. \\
Fish & Use Fish's own configuration syntax and startup locations.
It does not use a POSIX \texttt{PS1} assignment for its prompt. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Give a lasting edit a way back}

The practice stops short of modifying your actual home startup
files. On a machine you administer for yourself, first identify the
active shell and how your terminal launches it. Open its manual
to the startup rules. Inspect the existing file in an editor;
look for earlier definitions, especially for the name you plan
to add. Keep a working terminal open, save a separate known-good
copy under a new name that does not overwrite another backup, and
make only one small, clearly commented change. Open a second terminal
using the normal launch settings. Check both the changed behavior
and that ordinary tasks still work. Do not close the working
terminal until that new test succeeds. Terminal themes, key
interception, and scrollback are usually application preferences
instead, not failures of a shell startup file.

If an actual edit fails, the clean child commands above are one
possible rescue route; starting a fresh ordinary shell may just
read the broken file again. In the working terminal, inspect the
exact paths of the edited file and its known-good copy before
restoring. If you also made useful edits since the copy, compare
the two instead of overwriting blindly. Restoring a file fixes the
next shell that reads it, not necessarily the shell already showing
wrong behavior. In Fish use its documented startup bypass and syntax
rather than feeding Fish configuration through Bash. If something else
manages the file for you, such as a system administrator or setup tool,
ask where personal configuration belongs instead of bypassing the
manager.

If an editor saves a recovery file or an old-version copy
beside the startup file, know which one the shell actually
reads. Never paste a startup file into a public help forum
without checking for personal paths, service details, or
credentials. Usually the single changed line, the shell version
and the launch mode are enough to start diagnosing it. Leave
a short comment beside a lasting change explaining the problem
it solves. When that problem disappears, remove the change
deliberately and test a new session again; don't leave a
collection of mysterious lines for future you to guess at.

\begin{terminal}
diff -u ./candidate.sh ./candidate.sh
printf 'comparison status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

This preliminary comparison uses the same file to give an expected
status of zero. The \cmd|-u| option requests a unified diff, which
shows surrounding lines for differences. Compare a candidate with
a known-good copy this way before a real edit; status one means
differences, not a failed comparison. Higher status means trouble.
Check the status immediately after \cmd|diff|; another command
would replace it. The status reports comparison, not the safety of
the changes.

\begin{takeaways}
\item Try a shortcut in one shell before asking every new shell to load it.
\item A child shell isolates shell state, not access to files.
\item Retain a working session and a known-good copy for recovery.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{A shortcut with an honest name}\label{ex:43-name}
In the outer practice Bash define \texttt{whereami} to run \texttt{pwd}.
Inspect its definition, invoke it, then remove it. Confirm that lookup
no longer reports the alias. This exercise creates no files.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
alias whereami='pwd'
alias whereami
whereami
unalias whereami
command -v whereami
\end{terminal}
Expect the current practice path when invoking the alias. Final lookup
has no output and a nonzero status unless the name has another definition.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{A one-command setting}\label{ex:43-export}
Pass \texttt{UTP43\_NOTE} with the value \texttt{once} to just one
\texttt{sh} invocation without exporting it for subsequent commands.
Rerun the child without that assignment. Leave the variable unset.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
unset UTP43_NOTE
UTP43_NOTE=once sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "${UTP43_NOTE-unset}"'
sh -c 'printf "%s\n" "${UTP43_NOTE-unset}"'
\end{terminal}
The command prefix supplies the value to this invocation of the
external \texttt{sh} utility. Expect \texttt{once}, then \texttt{unset}.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Compare without installing}\label{ex:43-rollback}
Copy the candidate to \texttt{before.sh}; then write a new
\texttt{candidate.sh} with only a \texttt{second} prompt. Compare
the versions, syntax-check the candidate, and clean up both files
when finished. Do not install either file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
cp ./candidate.sh ./before.sh
printf '%s\n' "PS1='second> '" > ./candidate.sh
diff -u ./before.sh ./candidate.sh
sh -n ./candidate.sh
\end{terminal}
Expect two removed lines and one added line in the diff body. The
syntax check should succeed. Use the chapter cleanup below to remove
both fixtures. If you want to try before/after behavior, use a
separate child for each candidate rather than sourcing repeatedly
into one child: the replacement file does not itself remove an
alias previously loaded.
\end{solution}

Cleanup: check with \cmd|pwd| that you are in the outer practice
directory, not a different tab or shell. Remove only the two names
you created (omit \texttt{before.sh} if you skipped that exercise).
Then leave the empty directory and the practice shell. If
\cmd|rmdir| refuses, inspect what you left instead of recursively
deleting it.

\begin{terminal}
rm ./candidate.sh ./before.sh
cd / && rmdir "$lab"
exit
\end{terminal}

